
  <sn><md>### pE-pH Relationship

**Definition:**
pE is the **negative logarithm of electron activity** (pE = -log [e⁻]), measuring the **oxidizing or reducing intensity** of a system. pH is the negative logarithm of H⁺ activity. Together, **pE and pH** define the chemical environment and control the **form, solubility, and mobility** of elements in soil and water.

**Basic Relationship:**

The stability of elements depends on **both pE and pH**. Their relationship is shown in **pE-pH diagrams** (Pourbaix diagrams).

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20and%20Enviromental%20Chemistry/10.Water%20Pollution%20and%20Pollutants/assets/pE_pH_diagram.png)

**Key points:**
- **High pE** = oxidizing conditions (electron-poor)
- **Low pE** = reducing conditions (electron-rich)
- **Low pH** = acidic
- **High pH** = alkaline


**Redox Reactions Involving H⁺:**

Many redox reactions consume or release H⁺, linking pE and pH:

$$
\text{Fe}^{3+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+}
$$
(No H⁺ involved → pE independent of pH)

$$
\text{MnO}_2 + 4\text{H}^+ + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}
$$
(H⁺ consumed → pE decreases as pH increases)

$$
\text{NO}_3^- + 6\text{H}^+ + 5e^- \rightarrow \frac{1}{2}\text{N}_2 + 3\text{H}_2\text{O}
$$
(pE decreases with increasing pH)

$$
\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 10\text{H}^+ + 8e^- \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{S} + 4\text{H}_2\text{O}
$$
(pE strongly pH-dependent)

---

**General Rule:**

For a reaction: **Oxidant + mH⁺ + ne⁻ → Reductant**

$$
\text{pE} = \text{pE}^0 - \frac{m}{n}\text{pH}
$$

- **Slope** of pE-pH line = **-m/n**
- Larger m/n → stronger pH dependence

---

**pE-pH Diagram (Stability Fields):**

| Region | pE | pH | Conditions |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Oxic** | High (&gt;10) | Any | O₂ present, Fe³⁺, NO₃⁻, SO₄²⁻ |
| **Anoxic** | Low (&lt;5) | Any | O₂ absent, Fe²⁺, NH₄⁺, H₂S |
| **Acidic** | Any | Low | Fe, Al soluble |
| **Alkaline** | Any | High | Fe, Al insoluble |

---

**Natural Systems Ranges:**

| System | pE | pH |
|---|---|---|
| Aerobic soil | +12 to +14 | 5–8 |
| Waterlogged soil | -2 to +2 | 6–8 |
| Lake sediment | -4 to 0 | 6–8 |
| Acid mine water | +10 to +14 | 2–4 |

---

**Importance of pE-pH Relationship:**

1. **Nutrient availability:** Fe, Mn, N, S forms depend on pE and pH
2. **Metal mobility:** Fe³⁺ insoluble at high pE; Fe²⁺ soluble at low pE
3. **Toxicity:** As(III) toxic at low pE; As(V) less toxic at high pE
4. **Microbial activity:** Determines which electron acceptors are used
5. **Pollutant behavior:** Controls degradation and immobilization
6. **Water quality:** Affects color, odor (H₂S), and taste

---

**Example:**
In **waterlogged paddy soils**, pE drops (reducing). Fe³⁺ is reduced to soluble Fe²⁺, and SO₄²⁻ to H₂S. If pH rises, H₂S toxicity increases. In **well-drained soils**, high pE keeps Fe as insoluble Fe³⁺, preventing toxicity.

**Conclusion:**
pE and pH are **linked master variables** in soil and aquatic systems. Many redox reactions involve H⁺, so pE and pH change together. The **pE-pH diagram** predicts the stable form of elements, controlling nutrient availability, metal mobility, toxicity, and pollutant behavior.</md></sn>
